{"name":"Herbes de Provence","hex":"#3a5f49","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[],"entries":[{"title":"Herbes de Provence","blocks":[{"text":"Herbes de Provence is a mixture of dried herbs considered typical of the Provence region of southwestern France. Formerly simply a descriptive term, commercial blends started to be sold under this name in the 1970s. These blends often contain savory, marjoram, rosemary, thyme, oregano, tarragon, basil, bay leaves, and black pepper. Lavender leaves or buds are also sometimes included in North American formulations. The herb mixture is typically used with grilled foods, stews, and soups."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Herbes_de_Provence_02.jpg/330px-Herbes_de_Provence_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herbes_de_Provence","wikidata":"Q913548"},{"title":"Provence","blocks":[{"text":"Provence is a geographical, cultural region and historical province of southeastern France, which stretches from the left bank of the lower Rhône to the west to the Italian border to the east; it is bordered by the Mediterranean Sea to the south. It largely corresponds with the modern administrative region of Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur and includes the departments of Var, Bouches-du-Rhône, Alpes-de-Haute-Provence, as well as parts of Alpes-Maritimes and Vaucluse. The largest city of the region and its modern-day capital is Marseille."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Lavender_field_and_Mont_Ventoux.jpg/330px-Lavender_field_and_Mont_Ventoux.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Provence","wikidata":"Q101081"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Provence_in_France_%281789_borders_over_modern_Provence%29.png/500px-Provence_in_France_%281789_borders_over_modern_Provence%29.png?utm_